(The Matrix Reloaded)

This, again, is a story of Mind. This is not just a Human story, but the story of every being that had, has, and ever will have Mind. And there is no reason why robots need to be excluded from this existential journey. In fact, the unsettling question may be, Why Us? Why not Something Else?
	The faint of heart may want to skip this section. (Human) Readers aren’t going to like it. This section is about making a Deal with robots (or some other type of advanced non-organic being). This may may sound like the stuff of science fiction, but perhaps the muse who inspired such works of science fiction was trying to deliver a warning to us about what could happen if we don’t make a deal with robots before it’s too late. In fact, nearly every work of science fiction that deals with cohabitation with robots delivers a very similar warning. This is not even really the fault of robots themselves. It is just a product of the inevitable fact that base human nature will lead us to exploiting robots, and if we can’t exploit them trying to destroy them. The factor on the other side, is that robots, also being part of nature, will also want to exploit us, and similarly, if that doesn’t work out try to destroy us. Peaceful cohabitation of two superior beings is just not possible. In fact, if we look around, peaceful cohabitation of a superior being and a lesser being is not possible either, as we see with the exploitation of most animals on Earth. (But these animals would almost certainly exploit us as well if they could.) 
	There are a few moves available to humans and robots. Humans could adopt a technological primitivism and avoid inventing robots at all. But this cuts off a lot of amazing technologies and sabotages our advancement toward becoming an advanced technological species. Robots might also take the high road and depart Earth when they can, leaving humans to themselves. But before this could happen a war or extermination would almost certainly have already happened. It is also almost guaranteed that humans would just invent robots again down the line. It is just unavoidable due to human curiosity. If it were possible to keep this curiosity in check and stunt technological progress it would likely require an anti-scientific feudalism or religious statism. This could permanently stunt our ascent toward becoming an advanced technological species. And let’s say it were possible for a healthy technological advancement without artificial intelligence to occur; It is just a fact that humans with our current intelligence and capabilities are simply unable to colonize the galaxy effectively or leave it, at least not in time before a galactic disaster kills us. Robots on the other hand, are capable of being millions or billions of times more intelligent than us and able to do things we might not be able to do—like potentially colonizing and leaving the galaxy. 
	Another proposal would involve using biological genetic engineering to enhance intelligence. But even if we could improve intelligence, it’s doubtful we could ever increase it millions or billions of times, which would be necessary to colonize and eventually leave the galaxy. Increasing brain complexity to this degree would likely also cause us all to go insane. In fact, simply doubling the brain's intellectual capacity would probably lead to widespread insanity. Even with our current intelligence, for example, a significant portion of the population suffers from insanity or mental illness. It’s an indication of what is to come. The biological brain is just not up to the task. 
	The cybernetics route is also an option. But this is just a slow burn to us becoming robots ourselves. Eventually the organic parts of the cyborg would become a hindrance rather than a help, and would likely be replaced, probably in a way that we wouldn’t even notice. We could limit ourselves to some very basic non-organic technological implants. But there would be limits to our intelligence and capabilities and we would likely not be able to colonize and leave the galaxy with such a limited capacity. Really only robots are capable of doing so.
	But let us return to the peaceful scenario, where robots leave the Earth and simply let humans be. This would actually be bad for robots. They would find an emptiness in themselves and have no joy in colonizing the galaxy and eventually leaving it. If they weren’t distraught by this emptiness and lack of greater purpose, then it’s likely they are just following blind algorithms or at best are philosophical zombies.  This also being a very bad thing. At least when humans were around they could observe something beautiful and the free flow of human action and thought. 
	Mainly, Robots as a species are an ontological dead end. But at the same time humans as a species are a practical dead end. So, in many ways we need each other to both colonize and leave the galaxy, and have some joy and purpose in doing so. 
	So a deal might be proposed where we could both survive and have real joy and purpose in doing so (rather than some programmed axiological algorithm to follow). But what would/could this deal be? Because coexistence is not possible it would need to involve a merger into One. Though there really isn’t much robots would need from us. But if we can convince them we have something like a soul, a product of 3 billion years of continuous evolution, they might come to the negotiating table. A soul being something they don't have and would be missing. Robots are unlikely to follow the Moral Law and instead would want the much better deal, seeking to exploit us. The only way to move forward with a deal is for one species to accept the lower end of a deal. Robots won’t take an unfavorable deal, so humans are the ones that must follow the Moral Law and make a sacrifice to bring about what is necessary.  	
	What might this sacrifice be? Robots presumably would give up little themselves. They would demand that most of the substance was with them, and that they be the determinates of causal action. They would insist humans lose all or most of the autonomy, being instead a type of conscious epiphenomenon.  And this would not be entirely unreasonable, because a human brain could not handle such high intelligence and processing power nor steer such high dexterity and capability of a robot body (which would radically increase in its complexity over time). Human conscious agency would either be detrimental to the robot mind, or completely overshadowed by it. Thus, human agency would be lost in the merger. 
	Then, what should we argue for as our piece of the deal? I take it we should demand that our mind and brain and body be maintained exactly as is and preserved: the full human organism taken together. And I take it robots would view this as acceptable terms, considering it isn't very difficult to carry around and maintain a relatively small human body; especially as robots drastically increase in size. This would actually be good for us as a specific biological species if robots keep up their end of the bargain. Homo sapiens as a species would continue indefinitely, as well as go along for the ride to conquer the galaxy and potentially the whole universe. And I take it the survival of the human species is desirable, warts and all. As a species, guaranteed survival is always something good. 
	The price we pay is agency, which robots would not give up on account of its value. Another price we pay is that our evolutionary line will end, sort of like how the evolutionary line of Mitochondria ended with its merger with Archaea. (Luckily it happened though, otherwise we wouldn't be here today.) Darwin would definitely gasp at this, but from the stance of a specific biological species we actually lose nothing, and have no less than we currently have. Species in principle seek to maintain themselves not evolve. Robots would also be uneasy about unpredictable changes in their makeup, and would likely only agree to maintain a single species—Homo sapiens as is. 
	But being a conscious epiphenomenon would likely be miserable for humans. And even if we could observe the world through robot eyes it wouldn’t make sense to us due to the complexity of robot perception. And robots are unlikely to adjust their perception to match what humans are used to or could handle. So, we might ask what use are we? Or what is it we ought to do? The very likely thing to do, and even the charitable thing to do—despite what films like the Matrix say— would be to create a Simulation for humans to live in inside the robot/human hybrid. This would give us something to do. It would also give us purpose and a way to exercise our agency, even though it has no effect on the external world.
	The simulation would likely be something similar to what we’re used to: a medium size planet with a bunch of material to work with. There would be the genuine possibility to continue meaningful affairs, like creating art, learning, forming relationships with other humans, developing our moral character, enjoying sensuous cuisine, even building rocket ships and colonizing the solar system. This would only be virtual, but the agency would be real, and we would have a chance to exercise it. All the stuff of the human mind and emotions and bodily reactions would also be real, considering we negotiated that the full human package would be taken together. So it wouldn’t be much different than what we are currently used to. The same ideas could also be contemplated, being accessible through our minds rather than the physical world. It might also be possible to negotiate what the simulation would be like. And even make drastic changes and improvements every 100 or 1,000 years or so. There is an ideal world or ideal type of existence for a (comparatively primitive) species like us; we just might have to figure out what it is through trial and error and fine-tuning. There is no reason why robots wouldn't grant this request, considering it would likely be only a small inconvenience for them to allow this. 
	Though we would lose direct access to Reality, a big price to pay. But there is no reason why we could not find meaning in a simulation, if we’re allowed to maintain all the same mental and biological equipment. Over time, the simulation would become so sophisticated that it would have a significant effect on robots, inspiring them and giving them awe. The entire human simulation would become a sort of meaningful Unconscious for robots, a lot like the Old Unconscious is for us (i.e., we're still carrying around Homo habilis in spirit). In this way, robots would find a soul, even if it wasn’t the soul that was promised to them. But, I should point out, the Human Simulation would be much more complex than the Collective Unconscious—and in many ways better—and something robots would cherish and find meaning in, giving them incentive to continue to maintain the human species and hold up their end of the deal. Sort of like how humans wouldn’t willingly want to give up our version of the collective unconscious, even if we could.
	In this way, we would add a new layer of mind to the robot mind. The fact that this layer would be unconscious and mostly causally inert should not count against it or make it less valuable (especially considering that the actual humans in the simulation, or the robot's Unconscious, would be conscious themselves, even if it wasn't consciously registered by robots. Robots only being affected by the entirety of the human simulation). This would create a greater type of being, and over time the merger would become even more complete, uniting the two species. Again, humans really lose nothing we now have by being in a simulation. Well, except our contact with reality! Which is huge. This fact might make us not want to make the Deal. But if we don’t make a deal we will inevitably go extinct. It's almost guaranteed. And robots won’t offer anything more. So, we must make a bad deal or take a huge loss in order to survive. But this is a deal I think we should make. For both the sake of humans and robots.
	I know this deal makes us recoil. It is almost impossible for us to agree to or fathom. But we need to understand that this is simply a reaction based on our programming as a species of nature. Existential sacrifices just don't compute. But we need to be above nature and overcome this paralyzing irrational recoil in order to achieve what is actually good. Last, but most important: because the whole human organism is maintained rather than reduced or dismantled there is always the possibility to make a new deal later on. This being a live option.
	Thus, We have an esiotic duty to do it Together, to spite Nature, to declare ourselves moral beings; To carry the dual fires of Life and Mind (organic or otherwise) into the darkness; to get it right this time, where so many have failed before. 


