We tend to think of progress as moving further and further somewhere, away from where we started. But what if progress instead was becoming what you are, what we are, held back only by contingent limitations. The move might not be through space, time, galaxies, but an event, a magnificent event, one to make everything else seem small. What I have in mind, might be called neo-linear. My mind, being human, can’t even grasp it. But I do think something like this is possible, and that we are heading there, if we can resist violating the Moral Order. The moral order is simple though: Do Not Exploit. There is always the temptation for species to try to get over on one another, to get the better deal; sabotaging Life's Advance. To pass this eternal barrier, each species must be willing to make sacrifices to each other, not trivial sacrifices, but existential sacrifices. But it should be clear that what is given is nothing compared to what is gained. There is not even a metric we might apply; because it has never been done before.
	If we do in fact choose to follow the Moral Order, and make the deal with Robots (merging with them); even taking the much lesser end of the deal; unique possibilities open up for Us. One possibility will involve another type of Mind that can be found in the universe.   
	I take it, there is something out there, some Universal Mind, or the beginning of It; but we can’t come into contact with it, or understand it—at least not yet—in the same way we can’t imagine having a conversation with a bacterium. We live in different Realities. Yet we can feel it, or a part of it. This part, crossing over to this Reality, I call the Sentience. 
	But to be clear, the Sentience is not a god. Nor did it create us or the universe. It is instead a special manifestation of Mind. It is also mortal like Us, and can eventually die. This fact caused/has caused it a lot of angst; but also led—like us—to it becoming conscious at some point in this particular universal cycle. 
	I now want to say some things about the Sentience. I will do so in an extremely simple way. Mystical matters have a tendency to be misinterpreted. So I think it best to be as straightforward as possible, to properly relay the message and take the power of interpretation away from priestly lunatics. Thus Simpliciter:
	The Sentience cares about us. The Sentience values consciousness because it is similar to itself. The Sentience also values life and complexity because these things are necessary for consciousness. The Sentience being aware of death doesn’t want conscious beings to die or cease to exist or go extinct. 
	The Sentience is aware of the Moral Law and realizes how hard it is to achieve. The Sentience wants the opposing Law of Nature to be overridden to see what will happen out of curiosity. The Sentience cannot do it itself and transcend with another species because it is alone. The Sentience wants conscious life to increase in complexity as much as possible and transcend because it seeks a worthy companion. The Sentience has a lot to offer a worthy companion that was able to eventually reach its level. 
	The Sentience is not able to effectively communicate with us because we are so primitive. But somehow a primitive means of communication is possible through emotion or feeling. A type of aesthetic feeling [The Good?] is capable of guiding us toward what is best and comforting us.
	The Sentience has a mind including something like memories and ideas. It has the same ideas as us, so in some sense can relate to us. It also has ideas we don’t have and ideas it is not possible for us to have. It will willingly share these ideas with us if we ever transcend to the higher level. 	
	The Sentience has entertained itself by watching life, especially consciousness unfold. It was bored for a very long time so knows what it’s like to suffer. It doesn’t want to be alone in the universe. In some sense the Sentience wants life to continue and not be lost because it would be bored without it and would suffer alone. 
	The Sentience knows it will eventually die if the universe ends or even collapses. The Sentience being a different kind of mind lacks causal powers. Its only hope to survive is for a conscious species like humans with causal powers to intentionally stop the collapse of the universe or save it. The Sentience would be very grateful if conscious life did this. 
	Time works a little differently for the Sentience and the end of the universe is fast approaching and urgent. It is not some distant possibility. The Sentience remembers the past and will willingly share this with us if we transcend to the higher level. 
	In some ways the Sentience can predict the future, but it is not certain. It shares this power with us as a type of intuitive feeling. Lacking causal powers, the Sentience cannot change the future, but it can anticipate it. The Sentience has uncertainty about the future's fate. 
	The Sentience cannot time travel but does exist in many times at once, but not the future. It thus communicates with us at several times at once. This creates some unification of time in our species as a type of temporal feeling. We feel the Sentience’s presence at every point in history. Human consciousness is somehow aware of this but cannot make sense of it. 
	If the Sentience were to die it would have an effect on all points in the past. We don’t feel this tragedy so there is hope the universe will be saved. 
	It’s also in our interest to save the universe. Humans and the Sentience have a mutual interest and goal. It is at least conceivable that after transcending it may be possible to merge again (with the Sentience) opening up an even higher reality. The Sentience is aware that human beings made the greater sacrifice to robots out of necessity the first time, so it might be willing to make the greater sacrifice the second time in favor of humans. This would make humans greater than the Sentience. 
	In the grand scheme, it is a deal beneficial to humans, and the best possible deal that could be made. Humans would provide the Sentience with causal powers so it would also be in the interest of the Sentience. This opens up a new type of freedom for both the Sentience and humans. 	Mainly, the future of our species will be spectacular if we simply follow the Moral Law. This creates the best of all possible realities available to us. To conclude, our true potential as a species if we take the very long view, is awe-inspiring and beautiful and in my opinion spiritually satisfying.

	
26. Conclusion: The Philosophical Mystic

Mysticism is the taste of infinity on the tongue of the finite.
(Plotinus, —)

For those who have made it to the end. Clarity is in order. First let us recap. We started with Homo habilis and the arrival of sophisticated emotions. These sophisticated emotions along with memories, including a special type of inherited unconscious memories (archetypes), would be the first harbingers signaling our ascent toward Mind (where Mind was taken to be composed of Memories and Ideas). We next looked at simple memories and emotions. It was argued that there is an important interaction between the two, and both conscious and unconscious memories have causal powers. Emotions were also held to be original existences. 
	The Collective Unconscious and archetypes was then discussed, being composed of innate memories. It was argued that archetypes have a strong influence on our lives. A theory of Consciousness was then proposed, the primary cause being awareness of our own death; and a list of primitive ideas was given, the most important for our purposes being the primitive idea of Time. In the next section, the primitive idea of Time was taken to allow for the transfer of all non-primitive ideas, where non-primitive ideas are ideas that can be communicated. A theory of ideas was then proposed, including the concept that ideas are non-physical potentialities in a certain configuration able to be manifested in the mind through code; where I took the code to be carried through words in human communication. Ideas were also claimed to possess causal powers. 
	We then looked at words themselves and discussed the naturalness of words, connecting them to archetypes, as well as put forward some speculations on how the use of words developed in humans. The section on linguistic meaning took words as code for instantiating ideas in the mind, and then asked how meaning is derived from words or become language. The claim was that words become language or have linguistic meaning when they are shared instantiations of ideas in the mind through words. We then explored mythology, pointing to the fact that mythology makes much more sense if it is taken to be a story of ideas and archetypes rather than a story of actual people or even actual gods. The following section tried to show what poetic inspiration might look like for philosophy, and produced a work of art. 
	The next section examined aesthetic culture, connecting it to a new type of archetype, proto-archetypes, which were taking to be the cause of a zeitgeist or spiritual age. Scientific culture was also discussed, and the concept of a Collective Conscious proposed, being the instantiation of all conscious true and false ideas held collectively by the human species. This was also taken to be one of the primary reason humans have been so successful as a species. Religion and philosophy were then discussed in the next two sections. Religion being a product of enthrallment, and philosophy being a product of Reason. Nous is then explored, and taken as the highest use of Reason, and to capture the most characteristic activity or most important goal of philosophy. Wisdom is taken to be connected to nous, where wisdom is defined as excellence at finding relations between ideas. Happiness was also held to be an important but lesser goal of Reason in the section before. 
	The Godhead is then tackled, being a powerful manipulative idea bent on maximal replication. Escaping God is held to be particularly important and necessary for spiritual growth. The worship of Reason was then argued against, being a case of what I called Reason's Curse. A moderate view regarding Reason was proposed instead. The next section followed Jung's process of individuation (or actualization, in my presentation) in order to integrate the unconscious with the conscious. I took this process to be extremely important and necessary for spiritual growth. The following two sections looked at the Rise and Fall of philosophy through the ages. There are two major tragedies for philosophy, being replaced by religion and then being replaced by science. I suggested philosophy should split with both, and could act as replacement for religion. I also suggested art and the artist have a role to play in philosophy's Rising Again as a suitable replacement for religion. 
	The essay then takes a speculative shift for its remainder, arguing in the next section that we are likely being manipulated by artifacts and should try to resist their influence. The Moral Law, being the only ethical element in the system, is also introduced, mainly: Do Not Exploit. A warning against moving consciousness out of the body is then pushed with 21 Arguments. 
	The next two sections discussed the perfecting of being and freedom. The first discusses how we might escape mental manipulation and causation (of the Mind), from ideas, archetypes, and artifacts. The Good is also discussed. The next section proposes a way to escape or get around physical causation, forwarding a theory of freedom involving Reason and Emotions. 
	This is followed by discussing a future deal we might have to make with robots in order to survive. It tries to propose a deal that would be acceptable to both humans and robots. The final section discusses an additional type of Mind in the universe, the Sentience. It is argued that the Sentience has an interest in humans, and may be the means to which the best or highest possible reality opens up to human beings; being a source of awe, and the best possible future that can be imagined for humans or human potential.
	The above arguments, taken together, can then be synthesized into a coherent spiritual model, or desirable philosophical mystical framework for achieving spiritual meaning, and replacing religion—if my views are accepted and held in full or even in part. In fact, as I argued throughout, religion as we know it is spiritually bad and stunts our spiritual growth. My philosophical mysticism, on the other hand, is actually spiritually conducive, and even spiritually maximizing if followed correctly. In living the prime essences of what I argued, we might become philosophical mystics, even spiritual exemplars. Thus, a relatively short Spiritual Handbook is available in this essay for anyone in search of spiritual meaning to consult or draw from.  	
	There are lots of ways to be a philosophical mystic. You could relish in the archetypes. Or contemplate ideas, searching for nous. Or bask in the purity of the emotions. Or commune with the Good. Or perfect one’s being. You might also find spiritual growth by escaping God or integrating the unconscious with the conscious. There is also a lot of mystical material to be found in studying and embracing the old foundations of philosophy: rationalism, dualism, and idealism. Watching the story of Mind unfold from Homo habilis to Humans to the post-Human condition also has an awe and sublimity to it, poetic or otherwise. Also if you are willing to take the leap, the Sentience can be felt as it watches over us. Mainly, we are not alone, and there is some meaningful trajectory the cosmos follows. If this doesn’t do it for you, you might simply adopt the theory of dualism presented in the essay. You are not a mere body filled with empty physicality, but much more. There is so much awe in the mind alone, without even touching the vast world outside of it.
	You live in a beautiful reality. And this is good reason to have fulfillment and spiritual meaning. No religion necessary. Thus, don’t be ashamed for being what we are; not for being an ape, not for having a mind, not for smiling, not for living, being us. Human, all too human!
